<body>

    <header>
        <div class="container">
            <h1>Kinetic Energy</h1>
            <p class="subtitle">Physics • Classical Mechanics • Work & Energy</p>
        </div>
    </header>

    <main class="container">

        <!-- Introduction Section -->
        <div class="grid">

            <!-- Definition Card -->
            <div class="card">
                <div class="card-header">
                    <div class="card-icon">📖</div>
                    <div class="card-title">Quick Definition</div>
                </div>
                <div class="card-content">
                    <p><strong>Kinetic Energy (KE)</strong> is the energy possessed by an object due to its motion. It is the work needed to accelerate a body of a given mass from rest to its stated velocity.</p>
                    <br>
                    <p>Anything that is moving has kinetic energy. The faster the object moves, the more kinetic energy it possesses.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Characteristics Card -->
            <div class="card">
                <div class="card-header">
                    <div class="card-icon">⚡</div>
                    <div class="card-title">Characteristics</div>
                </div>
                <div class="card-content">
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Scalar Quantity:</strong> It has magnitude but no direction.</li>
                        <li><strong>Dependence:</strong> Depends on mass ($m$) and velocity ($v$).</li>
                        <li><strong>Translational:</strong> Energy due to linear motion of the center of mass.</li>
                        <li><strong>Always Positive:</strong> $KE \geq 0$ because mass and squared velocity are always positive.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Main Formula Card -->
            <div class="card formula-card full-width" id="main-formula-card">
                <div class="card-header">
                    <div class="card-icon">∑</div>
                    <div class="card-title">Main Formula</div>
                </div>
                <div class="card-content">
                    <p>The translational kinetic energy of an object of mass $m$ moving at speed $v$ is given by:</p>

                    <div class="formula-display">
                        $$ KE = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 $$
                    </div>

                    <div id="formula-explanation" class="explanation-box">
                        <strong>Explanation:</strong><br>
                        The energy is directly proportional to the mass ($m$). If you double the mass, you double the energy. However, it is proportional to the square of the velocity ($v^2$). If you double the speed, the energy increases by four times ($2^2 = 4$).
                    </div>

                    <div class="formula-actions">
                        <button class="btn-outline" onclick="toggleExplanation()">Explain Formula</button>
                        <button class="btn-primary" onclick="openFormulaLab()">Open Formula Lab 🧪</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Derivation Card -->
            <div class="card full-width">
                <div class="card-header">
                    <div class="card-icon">📐</div>
                    <div class="card-title">Derivation</div>
                </div>
                <div class="card-content">
                    <p>We can derive the formula using the <strong>Work-Energy Theorem</strong>, which states that the net work done on an object equals its change in kinetic energy.</p>

                    <h3>Step 1: Work Done</h3>
                    <p>Consider an object of mass $m$ accelerating from rest to velocity $v$ under constant force $F$ over displacement $s$.</p>
                    $$ W = F \cdot s $$

                    <h3>Step 2: Newton's Second Law</h3>
                    <p>Substitute $F = ma$ into the work equation:</p>
                    $$ W = (ma) \cdot s $$

                    <h3>Step 3: Kinematics</h3>
                    <p>Use the third equation of motion ($v^2 = u^2 + 2as$). Since the object starts from rest, initial velocity $u = 0$:</p>
                    $$ v^2 = 2as \implies s = \frac{v^2}{2a} $$

                    <h3>Step 4: Substitution</h3>
                    <p>Substitute $s$ back into the work equation:</p>
                    $$ W = ma \cdot \left( \frac{v^2}{2a} \right) $$

                    <p>The mass $m$ cancels out partially, and acceleration $a$ cancels out completely:</p>
                    $$ W = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 $$

                    <p>Since this work done equals the change in kinetic energy (from 0 to KE), we conclude:</p>
                    $$ KE = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 $$
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Variables Table Card -->
            <div class="card">
                <div class="card-header">
                    <div class="card-icon">🔤</div>
                    <div class="card-title">Variables</div>
                </div>
                <div class="card-content">
                    <div class="table-container">
                        <table>
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Symbol</th>
                                    <th>Quantity</th>
                                    <th>SI Unit</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>$KE$</td>
                                    <td>Kinetic Energy</td>
                                    <td>Joule (J)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>$m$</td>
                                    <td>Mass</td>
                                    <td>Kilogram (kg)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td>$v$</td>
                                    <td>Velocity</td>
                                    <td>m/s</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- Applications Card -->
            <div class="card">
                <div class="card-header">
                    <div class="card-icon">🚀</div>
                    <div class="card-title">Applications</div>
                </div>
                <div class="card-content">
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Hydroelectric Power:</strong> Converting the KE of flowing water into electricity.</li>
                        <li><strong>Automobile Safety:</strong> Crumple zones work to increase the distance over which KE is dissipated, reducing force.</li>
                        <li><strong>Pendulums:</strong> Continuous interchange between Potential Energy and Kinetic Energy.</li>
                        <li><strong>Rail Guns:</strong> Using electromagnetic force to accelerate projectiles to high KE.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

        </div>
    </main>

    <!-- Formula Lab Modal -->
    <div class="modal-overlay" id="lab-modal">
        <div class="lab-modal">
            <button class="close-modal" onclick="closeFormulaLab()">&times;</button>
            <h2 style="margin-bottom: 5px;">Formula Lab</h2>
            <p style="color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 20px;">Manipulate mass and velocity to observe changes in Kinetic Energy.</p>

            <div class="lab-controls">
                <!-- Mass Input -->
                <div class="control-group">
                    <label>
                        <span>Mass ($m$)</span>
                        <span id="label-m">10 kg</span>
                    </label>
                    <input type="range" id="input-m" min="1" max="100" value="10" step="1">
                </div>

                <!-- Velocity Input -->
                <div class="control-group">
                    <label>
                        <span>Velocity ($v$)</span>
                        <span id="label-v">10 m/s</span>
                    </label>
                    <input type="range" id="input-v" min="0" max="50" value="10" step="1">
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lab-result">
                <div class="result-label">Kinetic Energy</div>
                <div class="result-value"><span id="result-ke">500</span> J</div>

                <!-- Dynamic Math Display -->
                <div style="margin-top: 15px; font-size: 1.1rem;">
                    $$ KE = \frac{1}{2} (<span id="math-m">10</span>)(<span id="math-v">10</span>)^2 $$
                </div>

                <div class="visual-bar-container">
                    <div class="visual-bar" id="energy-bar"></div>
                </div>
                <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 0.8rem; color: #9ca3af; margin-top: 5px;">
                    <span>0 J</span>
                    <span>125,000 J (Max)</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- KaTeX JS -->
    <script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/katex@0.16.9/dist/katex.min.js"></script>
    <script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/katex@0.16.9/dist/contrib/auto-render.min.js" 
        onload="renderMathInElement(document.body, {
            delimiters: [
                {left: '$$', right: '$$', display: true},
                {left: '$', right: '$', display: false}
            ]
        });">
    </script>

    <!-- Interactive Logic -->
    <script>
        // Formula Extraction Logic (Simulated based on DOM content)
        // In a dynamic app, this would parse the markdown. Here we define the structure.
        const formulaData = {
            latex: "KE = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2",
            variables: {
                m: { name: "Mass", unit: "kg", min: 1, max: 100, default: 10 },
                v: { name: "Velocity", unit: "m/s", min: 0, max: 50, default: 10 }
            }
        };

        // UI Interaction Logic
        function toggleExplanation() {
            const box = document.getElementById('formula-explanation');
            box.classList.toggle('active');
        }

        // Formula Lab Logic
        const modal = document.getElementById('lab-modal');
        const inputM = document.getElementById('input-m');
        const inputV = document.getElementById('input-v');
        const labelM = document.getElementById('label-m');
        const labelV = document.getElementById('label-v');
        const resultKE = document.getElementById('result-ke');
        const mathM = document.getElementById('math-m');
        const mathV = document.getElementById('math-v');
        const energyBar = document.getElementById('energy-bar');

        // Max KE for bar visualization (100kg * 50^2 * 0.5)
        const MAX_POSSIBLE_KE = 125000; 

        function openFormulaLab() {
            modal.classList.add('active');
            calculateLab(); // Initial calculation
        }

        function closeFormulaLab() {
            modal.classList.remove('active');
        }

        function calculateLab() {
            const m = parseFloat(inputM.value);
            const v = parseFloat(inputV.value);

            // Update Labels
            labelM.textContent = `${m} kg`;
            labelV.textContent = `${v} m/s`;

            // Update Math Text inside Modal
            mathM.textContent = m;
            mathV.textContent = v;

            // Calculate KE
            const ke = 0.5 * m * Math.pow(v, 2);

            // Update Result Display
            // Format with commas for readability
            resultKE.textContent = ke.toLocaleString(undefined, { maximumFractionDigits: 1 });

            // Update Visual Bar
            const percentage = Math.min((ke / MAX_POSSIBLE_KE) * 100, 100);
            energyBar.style.width = `${percentage}%`;

            // Change color based on intensity
            if(percentage > 80) energyBar.style.backgroundColor = '#ef4444'; // Red
            else if(percentage > 40) energyBar.style.backgroundColor = '#f59e0b'; // Orange
            else energyBar.style.backgroundColor = '#2563eb'; // Blue
        }

        // Event Listeners for Sliders
        inputM.addEventListener('input', calculateLab);
        inputV.addEventListener('input', calculateLab);

        // Close modal on outside click
        modal.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
            if (e.target === modal) closeFormulaLab();
        });

    </script>
</body>
</html>
